8-## Key takeaways
9-
10-- The pinned SwiftNIO SSH baseline is tag `0.14.1`, commit
11-  `31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496`. Its exact resolved graph builds in
12-  release mode on macOS. It fixes the critical NIOSSH ECDSA advisory and resolves
13-  to patched Swift Crypto and SwiftNIO versions.
14-- At that source, every newly opened child channel receives a hard-coded
15:  `1 << 24` byte (16 MiB) target receive window. There is no public setter or
16:  observation hook. The client-initiated `_rekey()` method is also `internal`.
17-  Both require source-fork work and validation; neither gate may be waived.
18:- The most recent libssh2 release, `1.11.1`, is affected by six advisories fixed
19-  after that release. The auditable candidate baseline is therefore the exact,
20-  untagged upstream commit
21-  `a34302491c164d53c900fec9b3cbb050ecebe719` (`1.11.2_DEV`) with all six fixes,
22-  paired with OpenSSL `3.5.7`. This removes known release vulnerabilities but
23-  creates an explicit release-hygiene and supply-chain risk.
24:- libssh2 publicly exposes initial per-channel windows, remaining-window
25:  observation, and manual window adjustment. Its direct-tcpip convenience API
26:  still hardcodes the 2 MiB default, so a custom-window adapter must use the
27-  generic channel-open API and reproduce the RFC 4254 payload.
28:- libssh2 handles server-initiated KEX internally but exposes no public API for
29:  client-initiated rekey and no local byte/time threshold. Consequently both
30:  candidates fail the public client-rekey source gate before adaptation and both
31-  require the same sustained-transfer experiment.
32-- Neither source inspection nor upstream claims establish 500–1,000 channel
33-  headroom, extension-safe Apple packaging, bounded physical memory, or robust
34-  cancellation. These remain named, symmetric experiments. This audit does not
35-  select an engine.
