165-## SwiftNIO SSH source findings
166-
167:### Windowing and rekey
168-
169:The decisive window code is
170-[`Sources/NIOSSH/Child Channels/SSHChannelMultiplexer.swift`](https://github.com/apple/swift-nio-ssh/blob/31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496/Sources/NIOSSH/Child%20Channels/SSHChannelMultiplexer.swift#L211):
171-new child channels receive `targetWindowSize: 1 << 24`. The internal
172:`ChildChannelWindowManager` replenishes credit as the available window falls to
173-half its target. No parameter on public
174-[`NIOSSHHandler.createChannel`](https://github.com/apple/swift-nio-ssh/blob/31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496/Sources/NIOSSH/NIOSSHHandler.swift#L274)
175-sets, reads, or suppresses that behavior.
176-
177:Client rekey is
178:[`internal func _rekey()`](https://github.com/apple/swift-nio-ssh/blob/31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496/Sources/NIOSSH/NIOSSHHandler.swift#L511),
179-with a source comment that it is mostly useful for testing. The pinned source
180:does contain KEX state-machine support for reexchange and tests for initiated
181:rekeys, but there is no public trigger or automatic byte/time threshold. The
182:ReluxNIOSSH fork must expose narrowly scoped window and rekey controls rather
183-than replacing these gates with defaults.
184-
185-### Host key, authentication, and channels
186-
187-During client KEX, the state machine invokes the public
188-[`validateHostKey`](https://github.com/apple/swift-nio-ssh/blob/31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496/Sources/NIOSSH/Key%20Exchange/SSHKeyExchangeStateMachine.swift#L317)
189-delegate before user-authentication progression. `NIOSSHPublicKey` supports a
190-public OpenSSH serialization initializer that yields the algorithm and Base64
--
210-This intersects the [current OpenSSH defaults](https://man.openbsd.org/OpenBSD-current/man5/ssh_config.5),
211-but the upstream
212-[`README`](https://github.com/apple/swift-nio-ssh/blob/31cdc3c3391a10460dedf1170530cf651d2ca496/README.md)
213-explicitly describes a modern-algorithm, building-block library rather than a
214-production-ready client. RSA host/user keys, DH KEX, AES-CTR, and old MAC modes
215-are absent. That is a compatibility boundary, not a reason to weaken host-key or
216-cipher policy.
